  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 227 PM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\nDespite relatively cool and stable air at the surface, scattered\nstorms have continued to develop over central Nebraska. These\nstorms, fueled by decent elevated instability (1000-2000 J/kg\nMUCAPE), have resulted in several strong to marginally severe\nupdrafts (including 1.25\" hail in Holdrege around noon). Near-\nterm modeling has struggled to handle the subtle forcing \nresponsible for these storms, but the expectation is that \ncoverage will diminish this afternoon, with a potential uptick \nagain this evening over southeast parts of the area. Most \nactivity will likely remain non-severe, but a few strong to \nmarginally severe storms remain possible. Given the elevated \nnature of the storms, hail will be the primary threat. \n\nAdditional showers/storms may develop late tonight into Sunday\nmorning, aided by warm air advection in the low levels, although\nthis is unlikely to become severe given the timing. The highest\ncoverage will gradually shift eastward through the morning\nhours, with dry conditions returning for Sunday afternoon.\nLingering cloud cover will keep temperatures on the cooler side\nfor Sunday (highs only in the 70s for most). \n\nMonday is the main day to watch for severe weather. Ahead of an\nadvancing cold front, we should see substantial warming with\nmany areas returning to the 90s. This, of course, also yields a\nvery unstable environment as the cold front moves into the area\nduring the afternoon and evening. The latest Day 3 SPC outlook\nhas upgraded part of the area to a \"Slight Risk\" (Level 2 of 5),\nwhich seems appropriate. \n\nAfter this front moves through, the rest of next week looks \nrelatively wet and cooler than normal. Continued southwesterly \nflow aloft, combined with a stalled front just to our south will\nresult in daily chances for rain and thunderstorms. Details \nremain uncertain, but ML severe guidance would suggest that the \nsevere threat remains fairly low. Models vary on exactly \"how \nwet\" next week will be, but all of the global ensembles (EPS, \nGEFS, CMCE) along with their AI counterparts favor above-normal \nrainfall totals over the next 7 days.",
